<!-- IN PRODUCTION -->
<section class="ai-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="label">In production</span>
    <h2>Six systems, live, running unattended.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Each of these was specified, built, deployed, broken, debugged and instrumented by me. Dates are the day they went live and stayed live.</p>

    <ul class="ai-cards">
      <li>
        <h3>Always-on agent with tool access</h3>
        <p>A self-hosted LLM agent with terminal and file tools, running in Docker, reachable from three interfaces that share one memory and one config. Its source of truth is a versioned business knowledge base pulled fresh every morning.</p>
        <p class="meta">Guardrails were the design work: single-user allowlist, Computer&nbsp;Use disabled on a headless box, and a rejection of Docker-in-Docker sandboxing because mounting the host socket would have handed the container root-equivalent access it was meant to be isolated from.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Orchestrated content pipeline</h3>
        <p>Three agents on cron — research, writer, translator — feeding a human approval gate, then an atomic commit to git, a build, and a live page in three languages. Nine to five pieces end to end on the first full run.</p>
        <p class="meta">Measured cost: ~US$0.08 per original article, ~US$0.20 for a full Spanish&nbsp;→&nbsp;English&nbsp;→&nbsp;Portuguese set. Agents propose; a human ships. That gate is what makes a bad draft cost a rejection instead of a published article.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Per-caller LLM observability</h3>
        <p>Five services shared one API key, so the vendor could attribute spend to the key and never to the consumer. I built token-level accounting: one row per call, with prompt, output and <em>thinking</em> tokens stored separately.</p>
        <p class="meta">Thinking is its own column because on current models <code>in&nbsp;+&nbsp;out&nbsp;≠&nbsp;total</code> and the gap bills at the output rate. A one-word test probe logged 7 prompt / 1 output / <strong>108 thinking</strong> — an in/out table would have understated it by a factor of thirteen.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Lead pipeline and CRM, self-hosted</h3>
        <p>Inbound lead → CRM record → automated nurture sequence → status updates taken in free text over chat and written back to the CRM. Website forms, a gated members area with token auth, and lifecycle automation on the same rails.</p>
        <p class="meta">Verified live against real leads, including a plain-language message — "mark this one as lost, too expensive" — correctly resolving to a CRM update.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Analytics and a monthly close</h3>
        <p>An event pipeline into BigQuery, GA4, Search Console and marketplace data, reconciled into a monthly close that produces a roster, a P&amp;L and a metrics file. Every figure in the business is reproducible from a file.</p>
        <p class="meta">The first close corrected four things nobody had questioned, including a take rate everyone read as drifting between 28.6% and 29.7% — it was a flat 30.00%, and the denominator was silently tracking the country mix of each month's buyers.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>A three-language product surface</h3>
        <p>A static site with a build-time i18n layer, a gated members area behind token auth routed by the token rather than the page, twelve interactive tools rendered from two owned JavaScript engines, and a 328-item catalogue built from data.</p>
        <p class="meta">Marketing copy for the whole library reads from one data file, so shipping a tool is one edit — added after three sales pages were found selling products that did not exist in the buyer's language.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- DECISIONS -->
<section class="ai-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="label">Judgment</span>
    <h2>The part that isn't the code.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Anyone can now generate a working script. What a company is actually hiring is the decision about whether to run it. Four I'd defend in an interview.</p>

    <ol class="ai-decisions">
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>I upgraded two of four model consumers and deliberately left two behind.</h3>
          <p>A new model shipped, cheaper and stronger. Every published benchmark behind it measured coding, agentic and multimodal reasoning. Two of my four consumers were writing long-form prose in a specific brand voice — which none of those benchmarks measure.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">So two moved on the evidence, and two were left pending a side-by-side read of real output. Moving all four would have been substituting a proxy for the thing being claimed. The saving would have been smaller than one month of the server.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>I built the instrumentation to confirm a hypothesis and it disproved it.</h3>
          <p>The weekly cost spike looked like the weekly research agent. It wasn't. Research was the <em>cheapest</em> consumer on the box; the heaviest day was a Saturday, driven by translation, because a limit counted in articles was quietly costing two model calls each.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The design constraint that mattered: instrumentation must not be able to kill the thing it measures. The logger swallows <code>SystemExit</code> explicitly — it doesn't inherit from <code>Exception</code>, and a missing database password would otherwise have killed the writer mid-article.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>I stopped a translation agent from doing exactly what it was told.</h3>
          <p>Given market notes, the translator localised the race in an article — a Valencia marathon became a US one for English readers. Defensible on its face, and wrong twice: it trades high-intent, low-competition search traffic for a keyword every domestic coach contests, and it makes the page's own "same content, translated" declaration false.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Agent output that looks correct and is structurally wrong is the failure mode that survives review. The fix was a rule in the contract, not a better prompt.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div>
          <h3>I treat "the repo copy is not what the runtime reads" as the recurring bug, not a one-off.</h3>
          <p>Four separate incidents of the same shape: a config edited in the right file and read from a different one, a deploy script updating itself mid-run, a duplicated environment variable that was a last-writer-wins bug waiting for the day the two values differed.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Every server-side script now lives in version control and is pulled at invocation, with the fixed-path file reduced to a six-line dispatcher. It is an operating-model fix, not a code fix — which is the category most AI-adjacent failures actually fall into.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

